## Technology and conventions

- Runtime versions and dependency tooling: {{VERSIONS_AND_TOOLING_FROM_CONFIG}}
- Repository conventions: {{NON_OBVIOUS_CONVENTIONS}}
- Runtime/deployment/platform constraints: {{ENVIRONMENT_AND_AUTHORIZATION_BOUNDARIES}}

Use uv for dependency and environment management, pytest for tests, isort for
imports, Ruff for linting, and Black for formatting. Keep configuration in
`pyproject.toml` and commit `uv.lock`. Use a project-local `.venv`, exclude it from
version control, and run tools through `uv run`. Declare runtime dependencies
separately from development tools; do not depend on globally installed packages.

Set `requires-python` to the supported interpreter range and keep any
`.python-version`, formatter targets, and CI versions consistent with it. Pin
the development interpreter deliberately without narrowing library support
accidentally. Review dependency changes and their lockfile diff together.

Configure isort with its Black profile and the same line length as Black
(default 88). Let isort own import sorting, Black own formatting, and Ruff own
linting; avoid enabling competing formatting or import rules. Use type hints
throughout, built-in generics such as `list[str]`, and explicit return types.
Use Google-style docstrings for public APIs and non-obvious contracts, including
errors and side effects. Ruff is not a static type checker; preserve an existing
type checker and record its command if the project uses one.

## Development commands

Run from {{COMMAND_WORKING_DIRECTORY}} with uv and a supported Python interpreter.
Additional prerequisites: {{PREREQUISITES}}.

When adopting these defaults into a project with `pyproject.toml`, add missing
development tools with `uv add --dev pytest isort ruff black`. This updates the
configuration and lockfile; it is setup work, not a recurring verification step.
For a new project, initialize its metadata first and choose whether it is an
application or a distributable package. Preserve an existing build backend.

Routine commands after configuration and lockfile creation:

```sh
uv sync --locked
uv run --locked pytest
uv run --locked ruff check .
uv run --locked isort --check-only .
uv run --locked black --check .
```

Apply formatting with `uv run --locked isort .` followed by
`uv run --locked black .`. Configure exclusions so generated code, vendored
files, and virtual environments are outside these checks. Use
`uv run --locked pytest path/to/test_file.py -k case_name` for a focused test.

- Project-specific test invocation, if needed: `{{TEST_COMMAND}}`
- Static type check or additional checks: `{{CHECK_COMMANDS_OR_REMOVE}}`
- Application entry point: {{RUN_COMMAND_OR_REMOVE}}

Use `uv add` and `uv remove` to change dependencies. Use a deliberate lockfile
update for upgrades; normal checks use `--locked` so they do not silently resolve
new versions. `uv run` handles the project environment; a separate
`python -m pip install .` step and an installed pip module are unnecessary.
For a distributable package with a configured build backend, use `uv build` and
verify the resulting wheel in an isolated environment outside the checkout.

Distinguish commands found in configuration from commands actually run. Record
material environmental limits; never label an unrun command as verified.

## Verification and documentation

- Behavior and integration checks: {{CONTRACTS_AND_FOCUSED_CHECKS}}
- Unavailable dependencies: {{UNVERIFIED_BOUNDARIES_OR_REMOVE}}
- Authoritative documentation: {{DOCS_TO_UPDATE_WITH_BEHAVIOR}}

Write pytest tests around observable behavior, compatibility, and failure modes.
Use fixtures, parametrization, temporary directories, and controlled dependency
substitutes where they clarify the contract. Keep unit tests independent of
credentials, live services, wall-clock timing, and the developer's home directory.
Make integration-test dependencies and invocation explicit. Exercise cleanup,
cancellation, and partial failure where relevant; do not assert private details
merely to increase coverage.

Run focused checks during development, then the relevant project checks. Keep CI
and local commands aligned. Once those pass, repeat or expand verification only
for new changes or unresolved concerns. Update examples and public documentation
when behavior changes; report checks actually run and material limits.

Maintain project facts here after specialization. Preserve headings, section order,
and unrelated content. Subsequent instruction changes require explicit user
authorization for enduring requirements; do not record task progress here.

